[Music]  service classes these are classes like  intro to philosophy or critical thinking  or ethics the kind of classes that we  don't teach primarily to philosophy  majors but teach to just about everybody  who goes to college the things you find  in what we call the core curriculum or  Gen ed I think those are among the most  important classes that academic  philosophers teach and I'll tell you why  in just a minute and I'll tell you why I  think it's important not just for the  students but for the wider world and for  the people who are teaching them first  before I do that I'm going to try to  boil down what I have to say in just a  few points for those of you who like it  short and sweet  [Music]  so what is it that I want to tell you  here about service classes from the  perspective of somebody who has been  teaching them for years and years and  who knows what other philosophers have  to say about them there's five main  things that I want to say although you  never know once I start going in the  rant I may have a few other things and I  want to roll in there so here's the the  five things in sort of a nutshell one  there's a perception that these classes  are easy that they're not valuable that  they're kind of slumming I think those  are completely misguided sentiments on  the part of those who teach these  classes or who try to get out of  teaching these classes to these classes  are extremely important because they are  the ones where we actually justify our  existence to the wide world outside of  our little domain of philosophy 3 these  classes are typically the one or if at  best you know two or three chances that  we get as a profession to engage  ordinary people and to produce in them a  you know idea that philosophy actually  matters by teaching these classes well  for these classes really are among the  most challenging to teach well they  require a lot of attentiveness to  audience and to continual improvement  over time and the fifth point that I  want to make is that I actually think  that these classes should be mandatory  for professors in my field to teach I  don't think anybody should be able to  escape teaching these it should not be a  reward that we give to people there's  good reasons for this a lot of which  stem from the practice of philosophy  itself so now on to the rant  [Music]  all right so here is my rant this is  what I have to say about this topic  which I have very strong feelings about  to begin with a lot of my peers do in  fact have the perception that these  service classes like intro ethics you  know critical thinking they are really  not valuable and they're easy to teach  just throw some stuff together and toss  it out there you know assign the  students some some readings make them do  some exercises and boom you get it over  with and now you can get on with the  important things in your day I think  this is a fundamentally misguided way of  thinking about not just those classes  but what it is that we're doing as  philosophers as you know pedagogical  professionals you know even if just as  persons and our ethical obligations  towards other people I think that these  service classes are the most important  classes that 99.9 percent of us have to  teach including the elite professors at  elite institutions who probably haven't  taught these classes for a long time  because they managed to get out of doing  it so I have a lot of reasons why I  think this is the case one of them is  that you know these classes aren't  actually that easy to teach when you get  down to it in order to teach them well  you actually have to work pretty hard  and I'll talk a bit more about that  later on and what the ramifications for  that are one of the things I want to  jump into right away though is if we as  philosophers particularly those who are  in the Academy are bitching and moaning  and griping about the fact that we're  not being taken seriously by people  outside of philosophy and that they want  to cut our budgets and stuff like that  unless we're actually kicking in and  doing our work to spread philosophy  outside of the confines of the  department and you know and it's classes  we don't really have any grounds for  complaining and I you know I know  somebody is going to have an  issue with my saying that and I'm  perfectly fine with with you know  defending that I think that if you want  to complain about philosophy and its  role but you're not doing a lot of work  to improve that you're part of the  problem not part of the solution and you  know if you think about these classes  these are the opportunity that we have  to engage the the wide world outside of  academic philosophy this is where we  have all the other majors in our classes  unless we made the the dumb mistake of  farming out ethics to some other  departments because we didn't want to  teach it which I have seen some schools  do a terrible idea right we should fight  tooth and nail to keep those ethics  classes and we should make sure that we  fill them up with business majors with  fashion majors economics majors you know  psych majors everybody from everywhere  so that we get a chance to introduce  them to philosophy to some rigorous ways  of thinking to some of the stuff that we  at least give lip service to being so  valuable you know you you want people to  read Plato teach Plato you want people  to think that Descartes is worth  considering teach Descartes right don't  just gripe about it teach that stuff in  classes to people I'm not saying that  this is the only way that we can reach  people I mean look at me I I do work on  YouTube right some of my peers blog or  even write for you know various  magazines and they they spread  philosophy that way you know I'm  constantly trying to find new angles to  bring philosophy to many other people  outside of the Academy but we ought to  be doing the same thing inside of the  Academy it's crazy for us not to be  doing that I'll tell you another thing  too this is really when we teach a  service class one of the few chances  that we have to reach so many people you  know in so many colleges there may be  just one philosophy class required  there may be two philosophy classes  required and those are the only times  when people are going to be doing some  serious study of that discipline unless  they choose to do so on their own I have  had so many conversations with people in  my travels in my conversations in  restaurants in you know trains all over  the place where I talk with them and  they find out that I'm you know in the  past a philosophy professor now I tell  people I'm a practical philosophy  because it's easy to bring a lot of  things under that head and they say I  took a philosophy class back in college  or when I was in university and I'll ask  them well how was it what did you learn  what did you think about it and they'll  say it sucked my instructor gave us all  this stuff to read and then didn't  explain it and talked down to us and you  know you know gave us questions that we  had no idea how to answer and then  didn't help us at all with that sort of  stuff or my instructor just turned into  one big rap session and we learned that  philosophy is just you know blathering  on about your opinion  ad nauseam the circle the desk sort of  stuff I've heard so many stories like  that I've also heard a lot of great  stories where people say oh my gosh that  was an amazing class that's why I  continued to go on and read some more  Aristotle or some more Descartes or took  another philosophy class but think about  how many students we have turned off  from philosophy by not teaching intro or  ethics or critical thinking to the best  of our ability not in a sort of phoning  it in showing up getting that class done  moving on to our real work our real work  is there in that classroom every one of  those those students who takes a class  because it's required not because they  want to take it but because it's  required they count just as much as do  the majors the graduate students are you  know professorial peers who are gonna  read our papers maybe if they get around  to it we have a unique opportunity given  to us  semester after semester to reach minds  and show them that the stuff that we  thought was worth going to graduate  school to study really is valuable and  you know all we got to do is be there  for them and explain the stuff to them  and design the class as well and give  them the kind of assignments that  actually help them to learn and grow not  just make work stuff alright that's what  I think we ought to be doing this leads  me to the next thing these classes are  not the easiest classes to teach these  are the most challenging classes I think  it's harder to teach an intro to  philosophy class well than it is to  teach some graduate seminar to some  graduate students true you need to  really know the topic that you're  talking about actually from what I've  seen with with you know some graduate  professors maybe you don't need to know  it because you can just send the  graduate students out to do some  research and you call it a seminar and  you have them present their stuff when  you have to take play-doh or decart or  [ __ ] or any of the great philosophers  Merritt you know Mary Wollstonecraft Han  Arendt and you have to explain it you  have to take those texts written in ways  that are hard to understand even  sometimes for us you have to explain it  to people who don't really have a  background in that and are wondering  what the hell am i doing in this class  if you can do that you're really a pro  that's where the the real teaching takes  place and that's not just a matter of  what you do you know when you get up in  front of the lectern that's what you do  as you think about the stuff that you're  rereading for the hundredth time and  you're trying to figure out where my  students gonna run into the most  problems and you're 